<title>USA: U.S. Senator welcomes moves against Canada barley.</title>
<headline>U.S. Senator welcomes moves against Canada barley.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-10-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Senator Byron Dorgan, a North Dakota Democrat, said Tuesday he welcomed moves by the U.S. Trade Representative's office that he said would tighten the standards by which it monitors imports of Canadian barley.</p>
<p>&quot;The Canadians are now on notice that Canada does not have carte blanche to race toward the the record levels of 1993/94,&quot; Dorgan said in a statement.</p>
<p>Dorgan said he welcomed a letter sent by acting Trade Representative Charlene Barshefsky to Canadian Trade Minister Art Eggleton last week, which said the United States would consider taking action if there was a surge in imports of Canadian barley.</p>
<p>Barshefsky had earlier said the United States would consider taking action if imports reached 1993/94 levels, which were considerably higher than those of the past two years.</p>
<p>-- Washington Commodities Desk (202) 898-8489</p>
